kssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6091.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>09 - Shri Vimal Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: The Mulanayak (principal deity) is Lord Shantinath. Beside him stands the image of Lord Shri Vimal Parshwanath, a white-hued idol in the padmasana posture, 13 inches tall and 11 inches wide. Above the idol&#x27;s head is a canopy of seven serpent hoods.</p><p>Location: This temple is located in the Shravak neighborhood of Chhani, a suburb of the city of Vadodara.</p><p>History: Near the ancient cities of Darbhavati and Vatpadra, the town of Chhani was known in ancient times by the name Chhayapuri. A manuscript from Vikram Samvat year 1821 reveals that in those days Chhani had five beautiful temples. Over time, the temples fell into disrepair. It is believed that these temples were rebuilt into the present temple. In V.S. 1893, the ancient, appealing, and captivating idol of Shri Vimal Parshwanath was installed in the temple of Lord Shantinath, and in V.S. 2020 an image of Lord Mahavir was also consecrated there. In V.S. 1944, Seth Babulal Shivlal built a temple in Kothari Faliya and installed the image of Lord Kunthunath as the Mulanayak. In earlier times this town was a chhavani (military encampment), and so it came to be called Chhani. The people of Chhani are deeply devout. In every household and every Jain family of Chhani, at least one member has taken diksha (monastic initiation) - a matter of great pride for the people of Chhani. At present, more than 167 people from Chhani have become monks and nuns, and together with 58 mumukshus (aspirants for renunciation) from elsewhere, a total of 225 mumukshus have taken diksha here, sanctifying this pilgrimage site. Shrimant Sayajirao Gaekwad, the ruler of Vadodara, honored the town with the title &quot;the mine of diksha.&quot; This reflects the glory of this town - more than 20 acharyas have illuminated the Jain teachings (Jinshasan), including Acharyadev Shri Kamalsurishwarji, Acharyadev Shrimad Vijay Bhuvantilaksurishwarji, Acharyadev Shrimad Vijay Vijaymurgishwarji, Acharyadev Shrimad Vijay Navinsurishwarji, and Acharyadev Shrimad Vijaydev, among many other great acharyas whose birthplace was Chhani.</p><p>Other Temples: There is another temple here dedicated to Lord Kunthunath.</p><p>Art and Sculptural Work: This idol appears to be very ancient. The religious outlook of the Jains of Chhani village, and the Jain spirit woven into the pattern of their daily lives, is truly admirable. This is perhaps the only place where people worship using their own personal wealth (swadravya). Even the children of this place strictly follow the basic tenets of the Jain faith.</p><p>Directions: Chhani is 8 kilometers from Vadodara. Chhani is one kilometer from the railway station. Bus service, taxi service, and other vehicles are available. There is an ancient library, an upashraya, and a dharamshala here.</p><p>Scripture References: The name Chhayapur Parshwanath appears in the &quot;365 Shri Parshva Jina Nammala,&quot; composed by Shri Premvijayji in Vikram Samvat year 1655. It is believed that Chhatarpur Parshwanath is in fact the same as Vimal Parshwanath.</p><p>Hymn of Praise: O Vimal Parshwa, purify me and remove the impurities of my karma; dwell in the lotus of my heart; may all my faults be erased. My mind wanders, entranced by your lotus eyes. With loving devotion I bow to Shri Vimal Parshwa.</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Vimal Parshwanathaya Namah&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Vimal Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Vaniyawad, Post: Chhani, District: Vadodara, State of Gujarat, India. Phone number: 0265-2432858</p><p>The Fourth Birth - Kiranaveg and the Fierce Serpent Kaldarun: When his lifespan in the heavenly realm came to an end, the soul of Marubhuti was born as a son to King Vidyudveg and Queen Tilakavati in the city of Tilakapuri, on Mount Vaitadhya in the Sukachchh Vijay region of Mahavideh Kshetra. On reaching youth, he married a maiden named Padmavati; after his coronation, Vidyudveg himself took diksha and, through supreme spiritual practice, attained liberation (moksha).</p><p>A son named &quot;Dharanveg&quot; was born to King Kiranaveg. Once, Shri Vijaybhadracharya arrived there. Upon hearing just one of his sermons, King Kiranaveg was filled with detachment (vairagya). He immediately handed the kingdom to his son Dharanveg, and King Kiranaveg, together with Queen Padmavati, took diksha.</p><p>Muni Kiranaveg practiced severe austerities, wandering through forests and mountains. The soul of Kamath, having completed his span of life in hell from a previous birth, was reborn in that very forest as the fierce serpent Kaldarun. He bit Kiranaveg, who had been Marubhuti in an earlier life. Poison spread through the muni&#x27;s entire body. Though both their bodies had changed, one grew ever more equanimous while the other&#x27;s hatred grew ever more intense. Muni Kiranaveg let go of attachment to the body and remained steadfast in dharma-dhyana (righteous meditation), and so his body became free of the sensation of pain. Even when there is pain in the body, it has no effect whatsoever on the soul and the mind - and because of this, no trace of distressed thought (artadhyana) arises from the pain.</p><p>The Fifth Birth - Heaven and Hell: Muni Kiranaveg, who had been Marubhuti, died in a state of pure meditation and was reborn as a god in the twelfth heaven, while the serpent died and went to hell. One soul enjoyed divine pleasures for countless years, while the other suffered the terrible torments of hell for countless years. One soul continues to bind auspicious karma, while the other continues to bind inauspicious karma. As long as a soul remains unmindful, the binding of inauspicious karma continues unabated. Therefore, we have a particular need to remain vigilant. To break the chain of inauspicious karmic bonds, one should apply the remedies described in the Panchsutra, such as dushkritagarha (repentance for wrongdoing) and so forth.</p><p>Blessed indeed is the birth of those who have been granted the vision of Parshwa; great is the merit of those who have found a place at the feet of this wish-fulfilling gem! Even we, who are humble and possess nothing, have gained fame and good fortune through this name; every longing has come to its fulfillment since we found this life-giver!!</p><p>Hymn of Praise to Shri 108 Parshwanath: What a wondrous &quot;Manmohana&quot; (heart-enchanter) you are, captivating the hearts of all devotees; none can match the &quot;Jotingda,&quot; beholding whom the heart sways with joy. Listen, O Lord &quot;Shankhala&quot;! Destroy my enemy karmas. O Lord Shankheshwara! I beseech you, protect me! Protect me!! O dark-hued one (Sanvara).</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-011\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Lodhan Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-013\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Sanvara Parshwanath (Shri Shamala Parshwanath)</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:justify-between\"><p>१०८ पार्श्वनाथ तीर्थ दर्शन — श्री पार्श्वनाथ भगवान के तीर्थों का डिजिटल संकलन</p><p>Original source: Raj